<ProseLayout
  title="About Calcrafty: Who Builds It and Why"
  description="Calcrafty builds free calculators for money, health, math and everyday questions, with live data, plain-English answers and every step of the math shown."
  path="/about/"
  h1="About Calcrafty"
  intro={SITE.description}
  updated="2026-11-01"
>
  <h2>Why Calcrafty exists</h2>
  <p>
    Most calculator sites still work like it’s 2005: fill in a form, press a button, wait for the page to reload, and get a bare number with no
    explanation. Calcrafty answers as you type, uses today’s data where it matters, and explains every result in plain English, with the formula
    and your own numbers worked through step by step.
  </p>
  <p>
    We build fewer calculators and make each one the most useful page for its question. A mortgage calculator should know this week’s average rate,
    remove PMI at the right moment, and tell you what an extra $200 a month actually saves.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs Calcrafty</h2>
  <p>
    Calcrafty is built and run by <strong>{SITE.founder.name}</strong>, a bank auditor and coding enthusiast. Auditing a bank means checking
    how loans, savings and repayments are calculated, and seeing how often people are handed a figure without the reasoning behind it.
    Calcrafty was built to be the tool worth pointing people to: one that shows the answer, the formula and what to do next.
  </p>
  <p>
    Calcrafty is an independent site. It is not owned by a bank, lender, insurer or health company, and no lender can pay to change a result.
    Nothing on the site comes from or is endorsed by any employer, and it does not sell or promote any financial product.
  </p>

  <h2>How we keep results accurate</h2>
  <p>
    Every formula lives in one tested module and is checked against published reference values before it ships. Pages are reviewed by a person
    before search engines can index them. The full process is on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What Calcrafty is not</h2>
  <p>
    Results are estimates for education and planning. They are not financial, tax, legal or medical advice. For decisions with real consequences,
    confirm the numbers with a qualified professional, such as a lender, accountant or doctor.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>Found a bug, a wrong number or a calculator you wish existed? <a href="/contact/">Contact us</a>; we read every message.</p>
</ProseLayout>
